<h2>Section 38: the advance on costs</h2>
<p>Section 38(1) empowers the arbitral tribunal to fix the amount of the deposit — or a supplementary deposit, as the case may be — as an <strong>advance for the costs</strong> of the arbitration which it expects to be incurred in respect of the claim submitted to it. Where a counterclaim has been submitted, the tribunal may fix <strong>separate amounts of deposit for the claim and the counterclaim</strong>. In institutional arbitrations the same function is performed through the institution&rsquo;s schedules: bodies such as the Delhi International Arbitration Centre collect deposits against their published fee structures, and the tribunal&rsquo;s Section 38 power operates within that framework.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Deposit fixed.</strong> The tribunal (or institution) quantifies the advance, usually at the first procedural hearing, with reference to the amounts in dispute.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Equal shares called.</strong> Section 38(2) makes the deposit payable in equal shares by the parties — separately for claim and counterclaim where separate deposits are fixed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Default by one party.</strong> The other party may pay the defaulter&rsquo;s share to keep the proceedings moving — a payment it can recover in the final allocation of costs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Default by both.</strong> The tribunal may suspend or terminate the arbitral proceedings in respect of the claim or counterclaim concerned.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Accounting at the end.</strong> On termination of the proceedings, the tribunal renders an accounting of the deposits received and returns any unexpended balance.</div>
</div>

<h2>The default trap — especially for counterclaimants</h2>
<p>Because deposits can be fixed separately for claim and counterclaim, the sanction for non-payment is <em>severable</em>. A respondent that raises an ambitious counterclaim but declines to fund the deposit referable to it exposes only the counterclaim to suspension or termination; the claimant&rsquo;s case continues. Conversely, a claimant who stops funding leaves the respondent with a choice: pay both shares and press for recovery later, or let the reference stall. Parties sometimes assume non-payment is costless leverage in a fee dispute with the tribunal — in fact it is the claim itself that is placed at risk.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Budget at the start.</strong> The advance on costs is predictable from the amounts claimed. Pricing it into the litigation budget avoids mid-reference funding crises.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Paying the other side&rsquo;s share.</strong> It is an investment, not a gift: the payment is brought into the costs award under the Section 31A regime, where costs generally follow the event.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Counterclaim economics.</strong> A counterclaim attracts its own deposit. Inflated counterclaims raise the advance — and the exposure if they fail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Record everything.</strong> Deposit directions, calls and payments should be documented; the accounting at the end, and any later costs argument, turns on that record.</div>
</div>

<h2>Section 39: the lien on the award</h2>
<p>Section 39(1) states that, subject to any provision to the contrary, the arbitral tribunal has a <strong>lien on the arbitral award for any unpaid costs of the arbitration</strong>. The signed award may lawfully be withheld until the outstanding costs are paid. The provision protects arbitrators — who cannot sue conveniently for fees reference by reference — but it also creates an obvious pressure point at the most sensitive moment of the case: a party eager to enforce, or to start its limitation clock for a challenge, cannot obtain the award without clearing the demand.</p>
<p>The balance is restored by <strong>Section 39(2)</strong>. Where the tribunal refuses to deliver the award except on payment of the costs demanded, the court may, on an application, order the tribunal to deliver the award to the applicant on <strong>payment into court of the costs demanded</strong>. The court then conducts an inquiry and directs that the tribunal be paid such costs as the court considers reasonable, with the balance refunded to the applicant. The award is thus released immediately, while the reasonableness of the fee is fought out separately — the fee dispute no longer holds the award hostage. An application under this provision lies at the instance of a party, but not where the fees were fixed by a written agreement between that party and the arbitrators: a party that contracted for the fee cannot later invoke the court to re-price it.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Statutory answer</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Tribunal needs funds to proceed</td><td>Deposit in equal shares — Section 38(1) and (2)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One party defaults</td><td>Other party may pay that share — Section 38(2)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Both default on a claim or counterclaim</td><td>Suspension or termination of that claim or counterclaim</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Costs unpaid at award stage</td><td>Tribunal&rsquo;s lien on the award — Section 39(1)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Fee demand disputed</td><td>Deposit in court, award released, court fixes reasonable costs — Section 39(2)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note">Sections 38 and 39 operate alongside the Section 31A costs regime, under which the unsuccessful party is generally ordered to pay the successful party&rsquo;s costs, including arbitrators&rsquo; fees. The deposits are only advances; the final burden is allocated in the award. Parties should therefore treat every deposit receipt and fee communication as future evidence — the money mechanics of the reference frequently become the last contested issue in it.</div>
